      "text": "To solve for \\(2a + 3b\\) where \\(a = 2 - i\\) and \\(b = -1 + i\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Substitute the given values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) into the expression \\(2a + 3b\\).\n2. Perform the multiplication for each term.\n3. Combine the results to get the final answer.\n\nLet's start with the substitution:\n\n\\[2a + 3b = 2(2 - i) + 3(-1 + i)\\]\n\nNext, we perform the multiplications:\n\n\\[2(2 - i) = 2 \\cdot 2 - 2 \\cdot i = 4 - 2i\\]\n\\[3(-1 + i) = 3 \\cdot (-1) + 3 \\cdot i = -3 + 3i\\]\n\nNow, we add these results together:\n\n\\[4 - 2i + (-3 + 3i) = (4 - 3) + (-2i + 3i) = 1 + i\\]\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{1 + i}\\]",
